1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:05,180
You can test drive Orchard Core before you build anything of your own.

2
00:00:05,180 --> 00:00:09,085
This video compares the ways to try it, then runs the source code

3
00:00:09,085 --> 00:00:12,660
on your machine, sets up a site, and signs in to the admin.

4
00:00:17,260 --> 00:00:21,031
Try Orchard Core creates a demo site for you to explore.

5
00:00:21,031 --> 00:00:27,054
The documentation suggests the Agency recipe, which is a company website, or the Blog recipe.

6
00:00:27,054 --> 00:00:30,460
The site is deleted automatically the next Sunday.

7
00:00:31,210 --> 00:00:34,220
DotNest is a hosting service for Orchard Core.

8
00:00:34,220 --> 00:00:37,403
After you sign up, you create cloud-hosted sites.

9
00:00:37,403 --> 00:00:42,149
Unlike a Try Orchard Core site, these sites are not deleted periodically, so

10
00:00:42,149 --> 00:00:44,290
they can be used in production.

11
00:00:45,040 --> 00:00:49,633
With Docker, one command runs the Orchard Core image in a container.

12
00:00:49,633 --> 00:00:54,040
The dash p option publishes port 80 of the container on port 8080

13
00:00:54,040 --> 00:00:57,702
of your computer, so you open localhost on port 8080.

14
00:00:57,702 --> 00:01:00,930
A separate video covers Docker in more detail.

15
00:01:01,680 --> 00:01:06,330
Running the source code uses the repository that contributors work in, so you

16
00:01:06,330 --> 00:01:08,795
can read, debug and change any module.

17
00:01:08,795 --> 00:01:09,860
Let's run it.

18
00:01:14,060 --> 00:01:15,974
Install the .NET SDK.

19
00:01:15,974 --> 00:01:22,920
Then clone the OrchardCMS/OrchardCore repository from GitHub, and check out the main branch.

20
00:01:23,670 --> 00:01:30,105
In the src folder, go to OrchardCore.Cms.Web, the web application, and enter dotnet run.

21
00:01:30,105 --> 00:01:36,130
The command restores the NuGet packages, builds the projects, and starts the site.

22
00:01:36,880 --> 00:01:41,201
The console reports the addresses the site listens on.

23
00:01:41,201 --> 00:01:44,801
Open localhost on port 5000 in your browser.

24
00:01:44,801 --> 00:01:52,650
In Visual Studio, open the solution, set OrchardCore.Cms.Web as the startup project, and press Ctrl+F5.

25
00:01:56,889 --> 00:02:00,956
Because nothing is set up, the first request shows the Setup screen.

26
00:02:00,956 --> 00:02:03,869
It asks a few questions to configure your site.

27
00:02:04,325 --> 00:02:06,506
Let's name the site Test Drive.

28
00:02:06,506 --> 00:02:10,751
A recipe sets up a site with pre-configured features and settings.

29
00:02:10,751 --> 00:02:15,408
The Recipe list starts on Software as a Service; let's choose Blog, which

30
00:02:15,408 --> 00:02:18,415
provides a functional blog with CMS features.

31
00:02:18,888 --> 00:02:22,949
The Default Time Zone starts as the time zone of your computer.

32
00:02:22,949 --> 00:02:26,891
The database is Sqlite, which keeps the site in a file, so no

33
00:02:26,891 --> 00:02:28,774
database server is needed.

34
00:02:28,774 --> 00:02:30,068
Let's keep both.

35
00:02:30,521 --> 00:02:34,780
Under Super User, the account that has all the rights, let's enter a

36
00:02:34,780 --> 00:02:37,658
user name, an email, and the password twice.

37
00:02:37,658 --> 00:02:42,091
The password must meet the Password requirements that the screen lists.

38
00:02:42,551 --> 00:02:44,274
Let's select Finish Setup.

39
00:02:44,274 --> 00:02:46,805
It runs the recipe, which takes a moment.

40
00:02:46,805 --> 00:02:50,251
When it ends, the browser opens the home page of the site.

41
00:02:54,462 --> 00:02:57,060
This is the home page of the Blog recipe.

42
00:02:57,060 --> 00:03:01,039
The navigation bar shows the site name and the menu, and below the

43
00:03:01,039 --> 00:03:02,642
heading is a blog post.

44
00:03:03,108 --> 00:03:04,752
The admin is at /Admin.

45
00:03:04,752 --> 00:03:08,551
Because you are not signed in, the site shows the login page.

46
00:03:08,551 --> 00:03:12,462
For a test drive, let's sign in with the super user from Setup,

47
00:03:12,462 --> 00:03:17,338
although the Setup screen advises using it only for setup and disaster recovery.

48
00:03:17,805 --> 00:03:23,112
After the login, the admin opens on a page that says Welcome to Orchard Core.

49
00:03:23,112 --> 00:03:28,675
Its four cards are Create content, Customize theme, Manage users, and Learn more.

50
00:03:32,875 --> 00:03:36,753
The site you ran is an ASP.NET Core application.

51
00:03:36,753 --> 00:03:44,655
In Program.cs, AddOrchardCms registers Orchard Core, and UseOrchardCore adds it to the request pipeline.

52
00:03:45,405 --> 00:03:48,319
AddSetupFeatures makes Auto Setup available.

53
00:03:48,319 --> 00:03:53,505
Auto Setup installs a site on the first request from settings in the configuration.

54
00:03:53,505 --> 00:03:57,935
Without those settings, it does nothing, and the Setup screen appears.

55
00:03:58,685 --> 00:04:08,009
The project references OrchardCore.Application.Cms.Targets, which brings in the modules that provide the features, and the themes.

56
00:04:08,009 --> 00:04:15,345
Under src, they live in OrchardCore.Modules and OrchardCore.Themes, and the documentation is in docs.

57
00:04:19,545 --> 00:04:26,281
The site keeps its settings and its Sqlite database in the App_Data folder of OrchardCore.Cms.Web.

58
00:04:26,281 --> 00:04:32,629
The database is the OrchardCore.db file in App_Data/Sites/Default, where Default is the name

59
00:04:32,629 --> 00:04:35,285
of the tenant, the site you set up.

60
00:04:36,035 --> 00:04:40,744
To start over, stop the site, delete the App_Data folder, and run it again:

61
00:04:40,744 --> 00:04:42,547
the Setup screen returns.

62
00:04:42,547 --> 00:04:47,315
The ORCHARD_APP_DATA environment variable points the site to another folder.

63
00:04:49,315 --> 00:04:52,930
Orchard Core runs from its source code on your machine, with a site

64
00:04:52,930 --> 00:04:54,217
you set up yourself.

65
00:04:54,217 --> 00:04:55,505
Thanks for watching.

